<header><p class="st-kicker">county · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Leinster</h1><p class="st-meta">1171 CE – 1247 CE · capital Kilkenny</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The vast Anglo-Norman liberty in south-east Ireland won by Strongbow, whose division in 1247 created the counties of Leinster.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a>, deposed king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-leinster">Leinster</a>, sought Norman help and promised his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aoife-macmurrough">Aoife</a> and succession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Richard de Clare</a>, Strongbow. After Diarmait's death in 1171 Strongbow claimed Leinster, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II</a> granted it to him as a lordship under the crown in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-ireland">Lordship of Ireland</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a> and his successors settled knights, founded towns and built castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferns">Ferns</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-marshal-1st-earl-of-pembroke">William Marshal</a>, who married Strongbow's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabel-de-clare-4th-countess-of-pembroke">Isabel</a>, developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-ross">New Ross</a>, Kilkenny and many abbeys, and granted Kilkenny its charter. The lordship had its own courts, exchequer and seneschal.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The five sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-marshal-1st-earl-of-pembroke">William Marshal</a> all died without heirs, and in 1247 the liberty was divided among his daughters' heirs, forming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-carlow">Carlow</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-wexford">Wexford</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a> and part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-loigis">Laois</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butler-dynasty">Butler</a> earls of Ormond later held much of it.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1171 CE – 1247 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">British Isles</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.00%;width:1.51%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1171 CE – 1247 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kilkenny</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Richard de Clare, Strongbow, William Marshal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Anglo-Norman French, Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ireland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Liberty of Leinster</dd></dl></aside></div>
